Hello, world! 👋

If you are reading this, it means I have successfully figured out how to deploy a post on GitHub Pages. To be honest, typing this feels like a massive achievement.

For the longest time, GitHub felt like an exclusive club for elite developers. I would see people sharing their repositories and talking about "commits," "pull requests," and "merging," and I would just nod along, secretly having no idea what they were talking about. The interface looked complex, and the command line felt intimidating. I was terrified that if I touched the wrong button, I might accidentally delete the entire internet (okay, maybe just my own folder, but still!).

The Anxiety of the First Commit

My journey here wasn't smooth. I spent days just staring at the "Create Repository" button. I was plagued by questions:

What should I name it?

Should I make it public? What if my code is bad?

How do I even use Markdown?

There is a specific kind of nervousness that comes with putting your work out in the open. It feels like walking onto a stage without knowing your lines. I was worried about making syntax errors or looking like a complete amateur in front of the professional community.

Why I Finally Clicked "Publish"

Despite the butterflies in my stomach, I realized that everyone starts somewhere. The fear of not learning was finally greater than the fear of looking foolish. I told myself that this space isn't about being perfect; it's about documenting the journey of becoming better.

This blog post is my small victory. It represents me overcoming the "Imposter Syndrome" and deciding to just do it.

What's Next?

I am still a beginner, and I know I have a lot to learn. You can expect this space to be a mix of coding notes, tutorials I find helpful, and reflections on my growth as a developer.

Thank you for visiting my little corner of the internet. If you are also a beginner feeling too nervous to start, take this as your sign: Just commit your code.

Wish me luck! 🚀